= Cognitive load =

== Overview ==
'''Cognitive load''' concerns the demands a task places on limited cognitive processing resources, especially [[Working memory]].

The concept is widely used in learning and instructional design, but mental effort, task difficulty, information quantity, and cognitive load are not interchangeable.

== Limited processing ==
Complex tasks can require multiple elements to be maintained, related, transformed, or coordinated at once.

When demands exceed available resources, performance or learning may degrade.

Prior knowledge can reduce load because familiar information can be organized into larger meaningful chunks or schemas.

== Cognitive load theory ==
Cognitive load theory developed as an instructional theory.

It distinguishes load caused by the inherent element interactivity of material from avoidable demands caused by poor instructional design.

Older formulations also used a separate category called germane load; later theoretical treatments revise how that category is interpreted.

== Intrinsic and extraneous load ==
'''Intrinsic load''' depends on interacting elements that must be processed for the learning goal, relative to learner expertise.

'''Extraneous load''' is imposed by presentation or activity that consumes resources without serving the learning objective.

A difficult subject can have high intrinsic load even under excellent instruction.

== Measurement ==
Cognitive load is not directly visible.

Researchers use subjective ratings, secondary-task performance, physiological measures, eye tracking, and task performance.

Each measure captures only part of the construct.

== Design implications ==
Useful instructional strategies can include worked examples, removing unnecessary split attention, integrating mutually dependent information, sequencing complexity, and adapting support to expertise.

Reducing all mental effort is not the goal; productive learning itself requires processing.

